# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Caddyfile — TLS termination + reverse proxy for InnoVision (plan §B3.3).
#
# Caddy issues and renews Let's Encrypt / ZeroSSL certificates automatically on
# first start and redirects HTTP→HTTPS on its own; there is no certbot, no
# renewal cron, and no certificate path to configure here.
#
# ── BEFORE THE FIRST START (all three are operational, not config) ───────────
#   1. DNS: an A (and/or AAAA) record for {$SITE_HOST} must already resolve to
#      this host. ACME validates over the PUBLIC name — a missing record makes
#      issuance fail, and each failed attempt counts against the rate limits.
#   2. Ports 80 AND 443 reachable from the internet. Port 80 is not optional
#      for HTTPS: it carries the HTTP-01 challenge and the redirect.
#   3. `caddy-data:/data` must be a PERSISTENT volume (see docker-compose.yml).
#      Without it every recreate re-issues certificates and a few redeploys
#      exhaust Let's Encrypt's duplicate-certificate limit for a week.
#
# ── HOP COUNT — SET TRUSTED_PROXY_COUNT TO MATCH THIS FILE ───────────────────
# `src/lib/request-ip.ts` resolves the client IP by counting trusted hops from
# the RIGHT of X-Forwarded-For, so the app's `TRUSTED_PROXY_COUNT` must equal
# the number of proxies that append to that header in front of it:
#
#   Caddy ONLY (this file, direct to the internet)          → TRUSTED_PROXY_COUNT=1
#   Cloudflare tunnel (or another proxy) IN FRONT of Caddy  → TRUSTED_PROXY_COUNT=2
#   NO proxy at all (app reached directly on :3000)         → TRUSTED_PROXY_COUNT=0  ← MANDATORY
#
# The `=0` case is mandatory, not merely conservative: when X-Forwarded-For is
# ABSENT, request-ip.ts falls back to `x-real-ip` UNCONDITIONALLY — and that is
# exactly the header cloudflared strips. A direct-access deployment with the
# default (1) therefore accepts a client-forged `X-Forwarded-For` and mints a
# fresh rate-limit bucket per request, defeating every per-IP budget with only
# a once-per-process warning. Behind Caddy the header is always present, so the
# default of 1 is correct for this file's posture.
#
# ⚠️ THE NUMBERS ABOVE ARE MEASURED, BUT ONLY FOR A TWO-CADDY CHAIN. The old
# comment claimed the tunnel row was 2 without ever measuring it, and the
# measurement contradicts the old claim about how the header is built. Measured
# here with Caddy 2.11.4 (two Caddy containers in series, a backend echoing its
# headers, `trusted_proxies` configured as below):
#     direct → Caddy → backend, forged `X-Forwarded-For: 1.2.3.4`
#       backend saw {"xff":"1.2.3.4, 172.22.0.1"}   (forged value KEPT as prefix,
#                                                    Caddy APPENDED its peer)
#     edge → Caddy → backend, forged `X-Forwarded-For: 1.2.3.4`
#       backend saw {"xff":"172.22.0.1, 172.22.0.4"} (forgery DISCARDED, TWO entries)
#     edge → Caddy → backend, no forged header
#       backend saw {"xff":"172.22.0.1, 172.22.0.4"} (same TWO entries)
# Feeding those into `clientIpFromHeaders`: `trusted=1` picks the last entry
# (Caddy's own peer, i.e. the tunnel — correct for Caddy-only), and `trusted=2`
# picks the entry the tunnel wrote (the real client — correct for tunnel+Caddy).
#
# WITHOUT `trusted_proxies` (the pre-fix file) the same two-hop chain produced a
# SINGLE entry — the tunnel's address REPLACED the inbound XFF — and `trusted=2`
# then hit `parts[length-2] === undefined`, fell through to `x-real-ip` (which
# Caddy does not set: measured `xri: null`), and returned "unknown". Every client
# collapsed into ONE shared bucket. The `trusted_proxies` line below is what
# makes the tunnel row's `=2` true; it is not decoration.
#
# STILL NOT PROVABLE FROM HERE: the rows above model "another Caddy". A real
# cloudflared/nginx edge may write XFF differently (some send a single entry,
# some send two, some also set X-Real-IP). PROVE IT ON THE LIVE TUNNEL with a
# forged header before trusting the count:
#     curl -s -H "X-Forwarded-For: 1.2.3.4" https://$SITE_HOST/api/health
# and read what the backend logged. A wrong count silently funnels every
# student into one rate-limit bucket, which is the failure `request-ip.ts`
# warns about — so do not copy a number you have not reproduced.
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

# Global options. `email` is the ACME account contact — expiry notices go
# there.
#
# WHY THE QUOTES AND THE `:` — this line crash-looped the container (defect
# proven by the ops critic). A bare `email {$ACME_EMAIL}` with ACME_EMAIL unset
# OR set-but-empty expands to a lone `email` token and Caddy refuses to adapt:
#     Error: adapting config using caddyfile: parsing caddyfile tokens for
#     'email': wrong argument count or unexpected line ending after 'email'
# The old comment claimed Caddy "accepts" an empty value. It does not, and since
# docs/DEPLOY_VPS.md calls ACME_EMAIL "optional but recommended", the DEFAULT
# path was a caddy crash-loop that presents as an unrelated TLS/site failure.
#
# The fix is the `{$VAR:default}` form (empty default) PLUS the surrounding
# QUOTES, which keep the expanded value a single token so an empty expansion is
# a valid empty argument rather than a missing one. VERIFIED for all three
# states with `caddy validate` + `caddy adapt`:
#   unset      → Valid configuration; adapts with NO tls.email (no contact)
#   set-empty  → Valid configuration; adapts with NO tls.email
#   set-real   → Valid configuration; adapts with tls.email = the value
# An empty ACME contact is a real, supported posture (no expiry-reminder mail);
# it is not the same as a broken one. Set ACME_EMAIL in the project-root `.env`
# to receive expiry notices.
{
	email "{$ACME_EMAIL:}"
}

# The public site. `{$SITE_HOST}` comes from the environment (compose passes
# `SITE_HOST`, defaulting to the tunnel host) so the same file serves staging
# and a second institution with no edit. A bare hostname here (no scheme, no
# port) is what tells Caddy to obtain a certificate for it automatically.
#
# ⚠️ `SITE_HOST` has NO placeholder default, unlike `ACME_EMAIL` above, and it
# must not get one: an empty expansion leaves `{` as the first token of this
# block, which Caddy reads as a global-options block in the wrong place and
# refuses ("server block without any key is global configuration, and if used,
# it must be first"). That is why compose supplies the default
# (`${SITE_HOST:-innovision.zikr-i.uk}`) and why this file is only valid when
# SITE_HOST is non-empty. A wrong-but-present value is a cert for the wrong
# name (browsers reject it, loudly); an ABSENT one is a parse failure. Keep the
# compose default, or export SITE_HOST, but never leave it empty.
{$SITE_HOST} {
	# The single upstream. `app` is the compose service name — this file is
	# mounted into the caddy container, so it resolves over the compose network
	# rather than the host loopback. Keep this in step with the app service's
	# `127.0.0.1:3000:3000` publish: the loopback publish is for host-side
	# debugging (and for a tunnel that bypasses Caddy); the proxy itself talks
	# to the service name.
	#
	# `trusted_proxies` — EXPLICIT, not a default. Caddy's behaviour for an
	# inbound X-Forwarded-For depends on whether the immediate peer is trusted:
	# an untrusted peer's header is REPLACED, a trusted peer's is APPENDED to.
	# Leaving that to an implicit default means the header the app sees (and so
	# the hop count `TRUSTED_PROXY_COUNT` must match) can change with a Caddy
	# version bump. `private_ranges` covers the compose network and RFC1918
	# space, so the immediate peer is deterministic for both a plain Caddy-only
	# deployment and a tunnel/other proxy sitting on a private address. If your
	# tunnel reaches Caddy from a PUBLIC address, replace this with that exact
	# CIDR — otherwise its header is replaced rather than appended and the hop
	# count changes.
	#
	# `header_up -X-Real-IP` — strip the inbound X-Real-IP. MEASURED: Caddy
	# does NOT set X-Real-IP (a backend behind it saw `xri: null`), and it does
	# NOT strip one the client sent — a forged `X-Real-IP: 6.6.6.6` arrived
	# untouched. `request-ip.ts` falls back to `x-real-ip` whenever XFF does not
	# yield a usable entry, so that pass-through is a forged-IP vector on a
	# header Caddy has no reason to forward. Removing it means the fallback
	# degrades to "unknown" (one shared bucket) instead of a client-chosen key.
	reverse_proxy app:3000 {
		trusted_proxies private_ranges
		header_up -X-Real-IP
	}

	# Header handling — deliberately minimal, because Caddy's defaults are
	# already the correct ones here:
	#   - `Host` is passed through UNCHANGED by default. Do NOT rewrite it to
	#     the upstream name: Next's server-action CSRF check compares the
	#     browser Origin against Host/X-Forwarded-Host, and a rewritten Host
	#     makes every action silently abort (the login form appears to just
	#     reset). The app's allowlist (ALLOWED_HOSTS at BUILD, TRUSTED_ORIGINS
	#     at RUNTIME) is what makes the differing origin acceptable.
	#   - `X-Forwarded-Proto` / `X-Forwarded-Host` are set by Caddy. This is what
	#     makes the hop count 1.
	#   - `X-Forwarded-For`: MEASURED (Caddy 2.11.4) — the inbound value is
	#     APPENDED TO when the immediate peer is trusted (the `trusted_proxies`
	#     setting above, which covers the compose network and RFC1918 space) and
	#     REPLACED when it is not. Either way the LAST entry is Caddy-written,
	#     which is what `TRUSTED_PROXY_COUNT=1` relies on. The old comment said
	#     "appended by default" unconditionally; the replace case is real, and
	#     the hop-count header above records the measured entry counts.
	#
	# WebSocket upgrades (Supabase Realtime when it is proxied through here,
	# and any SSE/streamed AI response) are handled transparently by Caddy —
	# no `Upgrade`/`Connection` header juggling needed.

	# Body size: the app accepts 25 MB quiz source PDFs (50 MB per batch) and
	# OCR uploads. Caddy has no default request-body cap, but be explicit so a
	# future proxy-level limit cannot silently 413 the exam-critical upload
	# path. 60 MB sits above the app's own 55 MiB dev-proxy ceiling and the
	# 50 MB batch cap.
	request_body {
		max_size 60MB
	}

	# Health/logging posture:
	#   - `log` writes structured access logs to stdout (captured by the
	#     json-file driver configured in compose, which rotates at 10m/3 files).
	#   - The app's own /api/health is NOT used as a Caddy health probe. Caddy
	#     has no upstream health check in the core distribution, and one is not
	#     wanted: the Docker HEALTHCHECK in the app image is the single source
	#     of truth, and it deliberately asserts liveness only (never Supabase
	#     cron state, which would turn free-tier project pausing into a restart
	#     loop).
	log {
		output stdout
		format console
	}

	# Do not cache HTML. The app is session-heavy and serves personalized
	# pages; a caching proxy in front of it is a correctness risk, and Caddy
	# does not cache by default — this is a note, not a directive.

	# Response headers that the proxy should own:
	#   - HSTS is ALREADY sent by the app (next.config.ts, 2 years +
	#     includeSubDomains, no preload). Do NOT add a second one here; two
	#     HSTS headers are concatenated by some clients and the app's value is
	#     the one the security audit reviewed. Add `preload` only after
	#     HTTPS-everywhere is proven for every subdomain.
	#   - `-Server` removes Caddy's version banner. The app's own headers
	#     (X-Frame-Options, nosniff, Referrer-Policy, Permissions-Policy, the
	#     Report-Only CSP) come from next.config.ts and pass through untouched.
	header {
		-Server
	}
}
